 ## Architecture
 
-The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
+Every accepted submission becomes an immutable **revision** of the whole visit. A revision names the revision its author started from, so a visit's revisions form a graph rooted at the visit's creation.
+
+A revision with no children is a **head**. The head count defines the visit's state:
+
+- **settled** — exactly one head. That head's field values are the visit's current record.
+- **contested** — two or more heads. The heads are **competing revisions**; the visit has no current record until a coordinator resolves it.
+
+Nothing is overwritten and nothing is combined automatically. A worker's submitted visit survives as a unit, byte for byte, in both the submission log and the revision it produced.
+
+```
+          R1 (create, Mira)
+           |
+          R2 (observer name fix, Mira)
+         /  \
+R3 (species added, Tom)   R4 (cover estimates revised, Priya)
+         \  /
+          R5 (resolution, coordinator Dana — parents R3, R4)
+```
+
+Between R2 and R5 the visit is contested: R3 and R4 are both heads, both derived from R2. R5 leaves one head, so the visit is settled again.
+
+The store exposes authenticated submission, visit retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and resolution.
+
+## Submission Envelope
+
+A submission carries:
+
+| Field | Meaning |
+|---|---|
+| `visit_id` | Client-generated UUID of the visit |
+| `base_revision_id` | The revision the author started from; absent on a creation |
+| `idempotency_key` | Client-generated, unique per submission attempt |
+| `plot_uuid` | Catalog plot reference |
+| `fields` | The complete scientific field set, not a delta |
+| `author_id` | Resolved from the authenticated worker, not trusted from the payload |
+
+The envelope carries no field-level change description. A tablet needs to retain only the revision ID it last fetched for a visit, not a base snapshot for diffing.
+
+Revision IDs are assigned by the server. A tablet learns the ID of the revision its submission produced from the acceptance result, and uses it as `base_revision_id` on its next edit to the same visit.
+
+The server records `received_at` for every stored submission. Receipt time orders the history for display and audit; it never decides which of two competing revisions is authoritative.
+
+## Submission Outcomes
+
+A submission that passes validation produces exactly one revision, and the acceptance result tells the tablet which state the visit landed in.
+
+- `base_revision_id` names the visit's only head → the new revision becomes the only head. The visit stays settled; no review work is created. This is the common case for a worker editing their own recent visit.
+- `base_revision_id` names a revision that is not a head → the new revision joins the graph as a sibling of the existing descendants. The visit becomes contested.
+- The visit is already contested → the submission is still accepted, under whichever revision it names. A contested visit accumulates a third competing revision rather than blocking capture; a worker in the field is not told to stop and wait for a coordinator.
+- The submitted `fields` are identical to those of an existing competing revision from the same base → the submission is retained and attached to that revision instead of creating another head. Two workers who independently made the same correction do not generate review work, and both submissions are still on record with their authors.
+
+Resolution is the only operation that reduces a visit's head count.
 
